menu "iclforge hearth sink"

# THE TRUST ANCHORS OF UPDATES FROM GITHUB RELEASES (planning/esp32-ota.md, O10): the public certificates a
# pulled update's signed index has to chain to. A PEM file, relative to the project; embedded in the image.
# Empty embeds none, which is a build that cannot verify a pull and says so. A project that runs its own
# releases points this at its own CA (and ICLFORGE_FIRMWARE_UPDATE_BASE_URL at its own repository).
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_TRUST_ANCHORS
    string "Trust anchors for updates pulled from GitHub releases: a PEM file in the project"
    default ""

# Defaults chosen to be safe on an ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 rather than to match any
# particular DAC board: 5, 6 and 7 are ordinary GPIOs - not strapping pins
# (0, 45, 46), not the USB pair (19, 20), not the console UART (43, 44) and not
# the flash/PSRAM range. Any free GPIO works; I2S goes through the GPIO matrix.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_BCLK_GPIO
    int "I2S bit clock (BCLK/SCK) GPIO"
    default 5

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_WS_GPIO
    int "I2S word select (WS/LRCK) GPIO"
    default 6

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
    int "I2S data out (DOUT/SDIN) GPIO"
    default 7

# A SECOND I2S LINE. Off by default: nothing about a normal build needs it.
# Line 0 above stays the clock master; line 1, when enabled, is a slave whose
# BCLK/WS inputs are routed - through the GPIO matrix, not an external wire -
# to the SAME pin numbers line 0 already drives as outputs, so the two lines'
# frames stay sample-aligned. Only its DATA pin is new; see sink/i2s/
# audio_sink.cpp for why a second line has to run the exact same frame shape
# as the first rather than whatever a narrower layout would need on its own.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE
    int "A second I2S line is wired (1) or not (0), for layouts wider than one line reaches"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant even
        when it is off - see ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLAVE's own note on the same
        choice; a bool Kconfig option that is "n" emits no macro at all, only
        one that is "y" does. One ESP32-S3 I2S line carries at most 128 bits a
        frame - 4 slots at 32 bits (see ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLOT_BITS). A
        second, independent I2S peripheral doubles that ceiling, sharing line
        0's bit clock and word select so both stay aligned to the same frame.
        Needs a DAC or DSP that actually accepts two separate TDM lines - most
        simple stereo breakouts do not. Reconfiguring to bring up both lines'
        TDM mode has run on a DevKitC-1-N16R8 (GPIO matrix routing, mode
        switch, no crash) but not with anything on the wire to confirm the
        two lines stay sample-aligned - confirm that before trusting audio
        that depends on it. It is also two DMA buffers instead of one, each
        sized from ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_DESCRIPTORS x _DMA_FRAMES at this
        line's own ceiling width, in internal RAM: on the same board, over
        WiFi (ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP), reconfiguring to a six-channel
        unfolded layout with this on left too little contiguous internal RAM
        for the decode task's own stack, which failed to start - a real
        budget limit this project has hit, not a hang or a wrong answer, and
        worth sizing ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_DESCRIPTORS down before relying
        on this over a network source.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DOUT2_GPIO
    int "Second I2S line's data out GPIO"
    default 8
    help
        Only read when ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE is 1 - left visible
        otherwise for the same reason ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_BCLK_GPIO and
        friends are, regardless of which sink is even selected: one fewer
        Kconfig symbol that disappears from sdkconfig depending on another.

# THE SLOT WIDTH AND THE CLOCK ROLE.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLOT_BITS
    int "I2S slot width in bits (16 or 32)"
    default 32
    range 16 32
    help
        32-bit slots carry the decoder's 24-bit samples left-justified, which
        every DAC tried here accepts and a SigmaDSP requires, at twice the bit
        clock of 16 (3.07 MHz against 1.54 for stereo at 48 kHz). A TDM frame
        holds 128 bits, so one line carries four 32-bit slots or eight 16-bit
        ones: at 16 a 7.1 layout fits one line. With the second line
        (ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE) that doubles either way - sixteen
        channels at 16 bits, eight at 32 - so a 7.1.4 layout leaves the part
        only at 16 bits with both lines wired. The DMA queue is sized for the
        line's widest frame either way, eight 16-bit slots being the same 16
        bytes a frame as four 32-bit ones. Only 16 and 32 are valid.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLAVE
    int "The DAC supplies the clocks (1) or the ESP32-S3 does (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        0, master: the ESP32-S3 generates BCLK and WS from its own clock, which
        is what a DAC breakout wants. 1, slave: BCLK and WS are inputs on the
        same two pins, driven by the other end - how an ADAU1452 or ADAU1467
        that is the house's clock wants it; the sample rate is then what the
        peripheral is told to expect rather than what it makes. An int rather
        than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant.

# THE FRAME: whether a one- or two-channel layout opens standard I2S or the
# full TDM frame every other layout gets. See iclforge/sink_plan.hpp.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_FIXED_FRAME
    int "Every layout runs the full TDM frame (1), or 1-2 channels open standard I2S (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        0: one or two channels open standard I2S, the two-slot frame a stereo
        I2S DAC such as a PCM5102 or MAX98357A reads, and three or more open
        TDM at the line's full width. 1: every layout opens that full TDM
        frame, eight 16-bit slots or four 32-bit ones, mono and stereo
        included, with the slots past the layout zeroed. A TDM DAC set up for
        one frame shape needs this: an ESS ES9080 has its slot count, slot
        width and channel map written over I2C, and its PLL can lock to the
        bit clock, so a stereo play opened as standard I2S would change the
        bit clock under it and put the samples in slots it does not read. A
        mono layout rides slot 0 alone; map two of the DAC's outputs to that
        slot to hear it on both. A one- or two-channel play then holds the DMA
        buffers of the full frame: 16 KB at the default depth where standard
        I2S's stereo pair at 16 bits holds 4 KB, which an ESP32-C6 playing 2.0
        showed as 12,304 bytes less free heap. With
        ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE, line 1
        also runs for every layout, its slots zeroed while line 0 holds the
        whole layout, so its DMA buffers stay allocated for every play (see
        that option's note on internal RAM). The DAC's own I2C setup is not
        part of this example. An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a
        plain constant.

# THE I2S DMA QUEUE:how much audio the peripheral holds between the player's
# writes, as descriptors of this many sample frames each. Four of 240 is 960
# stereo 16-bit frames - 3,840 bytes, 20 ms at 48 kHz - which is LESS than one
# coded frame (1,536 samples, 6,144 bytes). A write only completes as the DAC
# drains, so once playback is under way the producer has the queue's depth, not
# the frame's 32 ms, between one write returning and the DAC running dry.
#
# Small on purpose for the flash and SD sources: every millisecond here is a
# millisecond of latency, and a deep queue would hide a decoder that cannot
# keep up, which is one of the things this example exists to reveal. A network
# source is a different case, and this is where its depth is set - the README
# records what a board needed.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_DESCRIPTORS
    int "I2S DMA descriptors"
    default 4
    range 2 64
    help
        With ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_FRAMES, how much audio the DMA queue
        holds: the default is about 21 ms. The player hands the sink a frame's
        six blocks together, 32 ms, and a 7.1.4 stream folded to 2.0 from a
        local source needs the queue to hold them and cover the next frame's
        decode too: twelve descriptors of 256 frames, 64 ms, for 16 KB more of
        internal SRAM. On a DevKitC-1 with no PSRAM, 714-tones.ec3 from the
        partition had 251 of 1,512 blocks reach an empty queue at the default,
        and none at twelve.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_DMA_FRAMES
    int "Sample frames per I2S DMA descriptor"
    default 240
    range 8 1023
    help
        1023 is the driver's ceiling for stereo 16-bit: a descriptor holds at
        most 4,092 bytes. The sinks use the largest descriptor at or under this
        that divides the player's 256-frame block, and add descriptors to keep
        the depth: a write that ends part-way through a descriptor can have the
        rest of it played as silence (see sink/sink_common.hpp).

# WHICH SINK. Kconfig rather than a -D cache variable, because the component's
# own CMakeLists is evaluated in IDF's separate requirements-expansion process,
# which does not inherit cache variables set by the project above it - CONFIG_
# values it does see. It also means CI selects the sink through the
# sdkconfig.ci it already overlays, rather than through a second mechanism.
#
# Read from CMake (main/CMakeLists.txt picks a directory), never from C++: the
# player does not know which sink it has, which is the point of the seam.
choice ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK
    prompt "Audio sink"
    default ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_I2S
    help
        Where decoded audio goes. The I2S sink reconfigures its own mode and
        slot count to whatever a layout needs, standard I2S for one or two
        speakers or TDM beyond that, or TDM for every layout with
        ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_FIXED_FRAME (iclforge/sink_plan.hpp), so a layout
        change over the control surface never needs a rebuild, on up to two
        of a part's I2S lines - correct on a part whose own TDM frame is 128
        bits (ESP32-S3, ESP32-C6). I2S DAC, wide TDM is the same arithmetic
        for a part whose one controller reaches the product's full channel
        target alone at a wider frame (512 bits on the ESP32-P4) - never a
        second line, see firmware/hearth-sink/main/sink/i2s_wide/audio_sink.cpp.
        The capture sink is what CI runs: qemu-system-xtensa has no I2S
        peripheral, so a real write blocks on a DMA that never drains. It
        converts with the same code (iclforge/interleave.hpp) and checks the
        result, with no peripheral behind it and no hardware ceiling to
        refuse against, so the source, the access-unit framing and the
        decode run as they do on a board for layouts a real line could never
        carry (a rendered 7.1.4's twelve slots, say). CI builds the other
        sinks and runs none of them.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_I2S
    bool "I2S DAC (standard or TDM, reconfigured dynamically)"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_I2S_WIDE
    bool "I2S DAC, wide TDM (one controller at the part's full frame width)"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    bool "Capture (convert and check, no peripheral)"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_NULL
    bool "None (count frames only)"

endchoice

# Which real sink the capture sink is standing in for. An int rather than a
# bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant; see the note on the source choice
# above for why the preprocessor is avoided here.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_CAPTURE_TDM
    int "Capture the TDM conversion (1) or the stereo one (0)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    default 0

# Whether the capture sink paces a Sendspin stream's timed writes as a DAC
# would, or takes them as fast as they come. An int so it reaches C++ as a
# plain constant.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_CAPTURE_PACED
    int "Pace a Sendspin stream's writes as a DAC would (1) or not (0)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        0, what CI runs: every decoded sample is written and checked as it
        comes, with no time attached, so no burst is ever late however slowly
        the emulator decodes, and the levels a Sendspin stream leaves are the
        stream's whole. 1: each write waits as a twelve-buffer DMA queue
        would and reports when it would have played, which runs the Sendspin
        player's timing under the emulator's clock.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_TDM_SLOTS
    int "TDM slots the capture sink emulates"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SINK_CAPTURE
    default 8
    range 2 16
    help
        How many slots the capture sink's emulated TDM frame carries. It has
        no peripheral behind it and so no hardware ceiling, which is exactly
        why CI uses it to check layouts wider than any real line here could
        carry - a rendered 7.1.4's twelve slots, say - rather than the real
        I2S sink, whose own ceiling now comes from ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_
        SLOT_BITS and ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SECOND_LINE (iclforge/sink_plan.hpp)
        and reconfigures itself to whatever a layout needs rather than
        holding to a number fixed here. Slots the layout does not fill are
        still written as zeros rather than skipped, the same rule the real
        sink follows. The layout (ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_LAYOUT) must have no more
        slots than this when the capture sink is what's built.

# WHERE THE BYTES COME FROM. A directory CMake picks, the mirror of the sink
# choice above; see main/byte_source.hpp.
choice ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE
    prompt "Bitstream source"
    default ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_PARTITION
    help
        CI runs three of the four under QEMU: the partition, the FAT volume in
        flash, and HTTP over QEMU's OpenCores Ethernet MAC (see
        ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK). The SD source needs an SDMMC host, which
        QEMU does not have, so CI builds it and runs its file layer
        (main/source/file_common.hpp) through the FAT volume instead. HTTP
        over WiFi is built and not run.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_PARTITION
    bool "Flash partition"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    bool "SD card (SDMMC + FATFS)"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_FATFS
    bool "FAT filesystem in flash"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP
    bool "HTTP over WiFi"

endchoice

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_FATFS_PATH
    string "Path to the bitstream in the flash filesystem"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_FATFS
    default "/audio/sample.ac3"


config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_PATH
    string "Path to the bitstream on the card"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default "/sdcard/sample.ac3"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_BUS_WIDTH
    int "SDMMC bus width"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 4
    range 1 4
    help
        4-bit if the card is wired for it. 1-bit works on any wiring and is
        several times slower, which matters less for the bitrate (448 kbit/s is
        nothing) than for the CPU time the transfer costs.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_CLK_GPIO
    int "SDMMC CLK GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 14

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_CMD_GPIO
    int "SDMMC CMD GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 15

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SD_D0_GPIO
    int "SDMMC D0 GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_SD
    default 2

# WHICH NETWORK carries the HTTP source. A directory CMake picks
# (main/net/), the same rule as the source and sink seams: the HTTP
# client never learns which. WiFi is what a board has. The OpenCores Ethernet
# MAC is what QEMU has - `idf.py qemu` attaches one to the host's network, and
# the guest reaches the host at 10.0.2.2 - so it is how CI runs this source end
# to end with no board and no access point.
choice ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK
    prompt "Network for the HTTP source"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP
    default ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    bool "WiFi station"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_OPENETH
    bool "OpenCores Ethernet MAC (QEMU only)"
    select ETH_USE_OPENETH
    help
        The MAC qemu-system-xtensa emulates. Not a device that exists on any
        board, which is why the driver refuses to run on one.

endchoice

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_WIFI_SSID
    string "WiFi SSID"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    default ""
    help
        Empty by default, so a board with nothing else stored listens for
        Improv at once (network.hpp) rather than spending the retries and
        the association timeout on a placeholder network first. Set this
        only for a fleet flashed from one image with one network in mind;
        Improv or `PUT /network` is how a single board is told its own.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "WiFi password"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    default ""

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_WIFI_RETRIES
    int "Quick retries before a join is reported as failed"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI
    default 5
    help
        How many times the station tries a network again at once, before a
        join at boot or over Improv is reported as failed. Bounded, so a wrong
        password is reported instead of the join waiting for good: a player
        that silently never starts is harder to diagnose than one that says it
        could not associate. At the default the report comes about 15 s after
        the first try for a network that is not there, and about 25 s for a
        wrong password.

        The station keeps trying after that, waiting 1, 2, 4 and 8 s and then
        15 s between tries, until it joins or is given another network. A
        network the board loses after joining gets the same treatment: these
        quick retries first, then the waits. A board whose access point
        restarts, or that boots before its access point after a power cut,
        joins once the network is back.

# WIRED ETHERNET, next to the WiFi station and not instead of it: a board with an EMAC and a PHY
# (the ESP32-P4X-Function-EV-Board's IP101GR) keeps the station for when the cable is out, and
# main/net/dual/network.cpp chooses between the two (planning/esp32-board-bench.md). The defaults
# are that board's; another P4 board with a PHY sets its own. The PHY driver is ESP-IDF's generic
# 802.3 one, which is what the IDF example for this board uses.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    bool "Wired Ethernet next to the WiFi station"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_WIFI && SOC_EMAC_SUPPORTED && SOC_EMAC_USE_MULTI_IO_MUX
    default n
    help
        Drives the chip's own Ethernet MAC through an RMII PHY. The board takes an address by DHCP
        on whichever interface has a link, prefers wired when both have one, and goes on by WiFi
        when wired has no link (and back to wired when it returns) with no restart. The
        preference between the two is a setting of the board (PUT /network-preference), stored in
        NVS like its name and its network.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_MDC_GPIO
    int "SMI MDC GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 31
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_MDIO_GPIO
    int "SMI MDIO GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 52
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_PHY_RESET_GPIO
    int "PHY reset GPIO (-1 = none)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 51
    range -1 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_PHY_ADDR
    int "PHY address on the SMI bus (-1 = find it)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 1
    range -1 31

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_CLK_GPIO
    int "RMII reference clock input GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 50
    range 0 54
    help
        The PHY makes the 50 MHz reference clock and the MAC takes it in on this pin.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_TX_EN_GPIO
    int "RMII TX_EN GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 49
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_TXD0_GPIO
    int "RMII TXD0 GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 34
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_TXD1_GPIO
    int "RMII TXD1 GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 35
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_CRS_DV_GPIO
    int "RMII CRS_DV GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 28
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_RXD0_GPIO
    int "RMII RXD0 GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 29
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_ETH_RMII_RXD1_GPIO
    int "RMII RXD1 GPIO"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NETWORK_ETHERNET
    default 30
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_HTTP_URL
    string "URL of the bitstream"
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SOURCE_HTTP
    default "http://example.invalid/sample.ac3"

# THE OUTPUT LAYOUT - libs/render/include/iclforge/render/layout.hpp. The
# speakers the room has, one per output slot; the stream is rendered onto them
# whatever it was coded as (iclforge/render/render.hpp). A stereo or mono layout is
# the decoder's own §7.8 fold; anything else is placed channel by channel, and
# a layout with height speakers has the stream's objects reconstructed and
# placed by their own positions.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_LAYOUT
    string "Output layout: a name (2.0, 5.1, 7.1.4, 9.2.4) or a speaker list"
    default "2.0"
    help
        A NAME is F.L.H: F speakers on the ring (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7 or 9), L
        low-frequency feeds (0, 1 or 2), H heights (0, 2, 4 or 6), and the
        slots come out ring, heights, LFE in Table E2.5 order - "5.1" is
        L C R Ls Rs LFE, the AC-3 order. A LIST is one token per slot in slot
        order: a location name (L C R Ls Rs Lrs Rrs Lw Rw Vhl Vhr Vhc Lts Rts
        Ts Cs LFE LFE2), azimuth/elevation in degrees ("30/0"; left is
        positive), or "-" for a slot nothing is on. "L,R,C,LFE,Ls,Rs" is a 5.1
        DAC wired in WAV order. The slot count must fit the sink: the real
        I2S sink reconfigures itself to whatever a layout needs, up to its
        ceiling from ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_SLOT_BITS and ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_I2S_
        SECOND_LINE; the capture sink is capped at ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_TDM_SLOTS.
        PUT /layout on the control surface changes it for the next play.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DRC_MODE
    int "Operating mode: line (0), RF (1), or as coded with no normalisation and no DRC (2)"
    default 0
    range 0 2
    help
        §7.7's two consumer modes, or neither. Line mode normalises to the
        stream's dialnorm and applies the transmitted dynrng words - what a
        player feeding a wide-range system does, and the default. RF mode adds
        compr, applied with 11 dB that put dialogue at -20 dBFS, and overload
        protection for a set-top box. 2 leaves the samples
        as coded, which is what firmware/baremetal/probe.cpp's references are
        measured against; sdkconfig.ci-render uses it so the rendered levels
        can be compared with the probe's to the digit.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DYNAMIC_RANGE
    int "Dynamic range a board starts with: standard (0) or late night (1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        The listener's choice (PUT /dynamic-range, and a screen's Preferences
        where the board has one), which NVS keeps; this is the value of a
        board that has none stored. 0 leaves everything to the operating mode
        above and to what a server sends: what a board did before the choice
        existed. 1 is late night: line mode's level with the stream's own heavy
        compression (compr) on top, which wins over a server's mode and
        compression settings for as long as it is on. It changes only streams
        whose encoder wrote the compression for it (compr words in AC-3 and
        E-AC-3, DRC decoder modes in AC-4), and never the level dialogue is
        heard at. planning/hearth-late-night.md has the measurements.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_STEREO_FOLD
    int "Fold for a 2.0 layout: Lo/Ro (0) or Lt/Rt (1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        §7.8.1's plain stereo, or §7.8.2's Dolby Surround compatible fold for
        a matrix decoder downstream. Only a two-speaker layout uses this.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_OBJECTS
    int "Object layer: reconstruct when the layout has height (0), never (1), always (2)"
    default 0
    range 0 2
    help
        An Atmos stream's bed is the objects' own 5.1 fold, so a room without
        height speakers loses nothing by playing the bed - and saves about
        10 ms of every 32 ms frame on this part, plus the reconstruction's
        heap (see ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_JOC_DOMAIN). 0 reconstructs exactly when
        the layout has a height speaker; 2 does so for any rendered layout,
        so a 7.1 room gets its rear surrounds from the objects rather than
        from the panned bed; 1 never does. A layout that folds never does.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_JOC_DOMAIN
    int "JOC reconstruction domain: QMF (0) or MDCT band (1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        Which domain the object reconstruction applies TS 103 420's matrix in.
        0, the 64-band QMF, is what the clause describes and what every stream
        from a licensed encoder needs; it allocates about 233 KB, which on this
        part means PSRAM. 1, the MDCT-band approximation, is correct ONLY for a
        stream this project's own encoder produced with the same setting, and
        allocates about 148 KB - which is how the QEMU shape, with no PSRAM,
        renders the height fixture (sdkconfig.ci-render). Not a choice for a
        board playing real content.

# AC-4, WHERE THE COMPONENT HAS IT (CONFIG_ICLFORGE_AC4, its Kconfig): a stream
# that opens with an AC-4 sync word is read by the AC-4 decoder in place of the
# AC-3 and E-AC-3 ones, whatever the source, and these say how. planning/ac4.md,
# D14b.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_AC4_CORE
    int "AC-4: core decoding (1) or full decoding (0)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_AC4
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        Core decoding (ETSI TS 103 190-2 clause 4.7) gives an immersive
        element's 5.X.2 core with A-CPL and A-JCC replaced or reduced, for
        low-complexity platforms. The Part 1 channel elements - 2.0 and 5.1 -
        decode alike in both.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_AC4_PARALLEL
    int "AC-4: decode each frame's per-channel stages on both cores (1) or one (0)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_AC4
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        On a part with two cores, with the decode task pinned to one of them
        (ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_CORE), a worker task on the other takes half of a
        frame's per-channel stages: each channel's inverse transform and QMF
        analysis, each output's synthesis and sample rate converter. The PCM is
        the same bit for bit. A play's location can say parallel=on or
        parallel=off.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_LANE
    int "AC-3 and E-AC-3: decode a frame's per-channel and per-object stages on both cores (1) or one (0)"
    default 1 if SPIRAM && (IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4)
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        On a part with two cores, with the decode task pinned to one of them
        (ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_CORE), a worker task on the other takes half of a decode's
        independent stages: each channel's inverse transform and overlap-add, and a JOC
        reconstruction's bed analyses and objects. It is a task with its stack in PSRAM where
        there is PSRAM, at the decode task's priority. The PCM is the same bit for bit.
        Measured on an ESP32-S3 (planning/esp32-714-realtime.md): objects 7.1.4 ... see the
        board page for the figures.

        On by default only where there is PSRAM. Without it the task's stack (12 KB) and its
        control block come out of internal RAM, which is what the player, the decoder and Wi-Fi
        share. Under QEMU (no PSRAM) CI's object render has 10 KB of internal RAM left at its
        worst moment, and its 7.1.4 stream set 24 KB against a floor of 20,480 bytes; the
        lane fits in neither. Set it to 1 on a part with no PSRAM that has the room. CI runs it
        in sdkconfig.ci-lane, over QEMU's emulated PSRAM.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_AC4_PCM_HASH
    int "AC-4: hash every decoded sample (1) or not (0)"
    depends on ICLFORGE_AC4
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        The play's end line then carries a hash of the bit pattern of every
        sample the decoder handed over (ac4.pcm_hash), the probe's own FNV-1a,
        which is what says whether the float output equals the host's. The
        time it takes is measured and taken back out of the decoder's.

# THE PLAYER'S TWO TASKS - see firmware/esp-idf/iclforge/include/iclforge/player.hpp. The
# fetch task reads the source into a ring; the decode task drains the ring,
# decodes and writes to the sink. WiFi and TCP/IP run on core 0, so the fetch
# task goes beside them and the decode task has core 1 to itself. -1 lets the
# scheduler choose, for a build with one core.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_FETCH_CORE
    int "Core for the fetch task (-1 = any)"
    default 0
    range -1 1

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_CORE
    int "Core for the decode task (-1 = any)"
    default 1
    range -1 1

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_RING_BYTES
    int "Ring between fetch and decode, bytes of bitstream"
    default 32768
    range 4096 262144
    help
        How long a stall in the source the DAC never hears: 32 KB is 0.57 s of
        a 448 kbit/s stream. In PSRAM when the part has it and the option
        below says so, in internal SRAM otherwise.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_RING_IN_PSRAM
    int "Put the ring in PSRAM when present (1) or internal SRAM (0)"
    default 1
    range 0 1
    help
        An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant, the
        same reason the capture sink's choice is one.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack for the decode task, bytes"
    default 32768
    range 12288 65536
    help
        The player reports what the decode actually used as
        stream.decode_stack_free at the end of a run: this minus that. Measured
        under QEMU on 2026-09-10, an AC-3 5.1 decode left 20,604 of 32,768
        spare. The default keeps the probe's margin; a shape that is short of
        heap can spend some of it, knowing the number.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_STACK_IN_PSRAM
    int "Put the decode task's stack in PSRAM when present (1) or internal SRAM (0)"
    default 1 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && SPIRAM
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        An int rather than a bool so it reaches C++ as a plain constant.
        For a part whose internal RAM is what Wi-Fi leaves it: on an
        ESP32-S3 with Wi-Fi and the player up no internal block is over
        31,744 bytes, which is less than this option's 32,768-byte stack
        and less than an AC-4 5.1 play with A-CPL needs (planning/ac4.md,
        D14c). Needs CONFIG_SPIRAM and CONFIG_FREERTOS_TASK_CREATE_ALLOW_EXT_MEM;
        without PSRAM present the stack is internal as before. On by default
        for an ESP32-S3 image with PSRAM, whether or not it has AC-4: it was
        on only with AC-4 until 2026-10-12, when an HTTP POST /play on the
        S3 release image, which has none, could not start its decode task
        (planning/esp32-platform-profiles.md). The stack is then read
        through the data cache: measured on a board, a 2.0 A-SPX frame took
        5% longer and AC-3 and E-AC-3 frames the same
        (docs/platforms/bare-metal/esp32-s3.md, AC-4).

# A PLAY'S START - PlayerConfig::hold_first_unit. The first access unit held
# until the second has decoded, so the sink starts with two frames queued;
# planning/esp32-714-realtime.md, decision 14, has why 7.1.4 over WiFi wants it.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_HOLD_FIRST_UNIT
    int "Hold a play's first unit until its second has decoded (1) or not (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        A play's first frames decode more slowly than the rest, and with only
        the first frame queued a 7.1.4 stream played over WiFi ran the DAC dry
        in them. Holding the first unit costs 32 ms before a play is heard, and
        a copy of the unit while it is held: 1 KB a channel for each of its six
        blocks, in PSRAM when the part has it.

# A PLAY'S MEMORY FLOOR. What a play asks of internal RAM is not known until it
# has asked: the ring, the decode task's stack, the I2S queue and the decoder's
# state are taken one after another, and a part that runs out partway through
# is met as an abort() inside the decoder, which reboots the board. This is the
# check made before any of that, with the figure the build was sized for.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PLAY_MIN_FREE_BYTES
    int "Internal RAM a play needs free once its source is open, bytes (0 = no check)"
    default 0
    range 0 1000000
    help
        A play started by POST /play, or the one configured at boot, is
        refused when less than this much internal RAM is free once its source
        is open: the play fails with the reason "memory" and the figure that
        was free as its error, GET /status says so, the console says how much
        was needed, and the board plays on. It is the sum of what the play
        takes before its decoder starts (the ring, the decode task's stack,
        the fetch task's, the I2S queue) and the least internal RAM any
        stream's decoder has asked for. A floor and not a fit: a wider stream
        asks for more, and a part under the floor cannot play any. 0 makes no
        check, which is right where the ring and the stacks are in PSRAM.
        Sendspin plays do not pass through it.

# THE CONTROL SURFACE - iclforge::Control, firmware/esp-idf/iclforge/include/iclforge/
# control.hpp: GET /status, POST /play with a location, POST /stop, POST /volume.
# Only worth having where there is a network, so 0 (off) by default; the HTTP
# source's configurations turn it on.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_CONTROL_PORT
    int "TCP port for the REST control surface (0 = none)"
    default 0
    range 0 65535

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_MAX_LAPS
    int "Stop after this many passes through the stream (0 = never stop)"
    default 0
    help
        0 plays forever, which is what a demo on a board should do. CI sets a
        small number so the application finishes and prints a verdict something
        can gate on.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_REPORT_EVERY_FRAMES
    int "Print a progress line every this many frames (0 = only at the end of a pass)"
    default 0
    help
        The timing line is printed at the end of each pass through the stream.
        A source that cannot rewind - HTTP - makes exactly one pass, which for
        a long stream means one line after minutes of silence on the console.
        A non-zero value prints the same figures, cumulative, every N frames as
        well; 125 is every four seconds at 48 kHz. A Sendspin stream prints a
        sendspin.progress line every N chunks, with the internal heap free.

# What the player and the board state about themselves in every build, whether or not it has a Sendspin player:
# main/settings.cpp reads the trim and main/board_caps.cpp the two limits. They are here, outside the menu below,
# because a symbol in a menu that depends on ICLFORGE_SENDSPIN is undeclared in a build without it, and a source
# that names it then fails to compile (the ESP32-S3 capture-sink build of CI's QEMU leg).
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SPEAKER_TRIM
    string "The listener's level and distance for each speaker, as a build starts with them"
    default ""
    help
        What a board has before anyone sets anything: a PUT /trim body on one line,
        statements separated by semicolons - "L level=-1.5 distance=2.4; R level=-1.5
        distance=2.4". Empty is every speaker at 0 dB with no distance, which is
        what a stream plays through unchanged. What a person sets over the control
        surface or on the screen is kept in NVS and replaces this whole.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_MAX_CODED_CHANNELS
    int "Widest syncframe decoded: full-bandwidth channels plus LFE (0 = no limit)"
    default 0
    range 0 8
    help
        _iclforge_player@v1 sends the server's own stream, at whatever
        channel count its programme codes: `outputs.count` bounds routing,
        not the source, so this is the only thing that stops a wider
        syncframe reaching the decoder. Past this count a syncframe is
        refused before a decoder is opened for it - the console prints why,
        once per stream - rather than decoded: the decoder's own scratch
        scales with the channel count, and on a part with no PSRAM and a
        Sendspin ring resident, running out partway through a 5.1 open can
        fail an allocation the heap has no room left to recover from, which
        aborts the board. On an ESP32-C6 with sdkconfig.sendspin-c6's 48 KB
        ring, 2.0 played every fixture measured; the AC-3 and E-AC-3 5.1
        fixtures each aborted within about 10 to 12 seconds, so
        sdkconfig.sendspin-c6 sets this to 2.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_MAX_OBJECTS
    int "E-AC-3 objects reconstructed and placed in real time (-1 = none stated, 0 = none)"
    default 0 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    default 0 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    default 12 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default -1
    range -1 15
    help
        What the board's support object says about objects, so that a server which wants a
        stream's objects placed can send it PCM rendered on its own side when the stream has more
        than this (planning/hearth-sendspin-extension.md): decoded on the board, a stream past it
        plays its bed alone. 0 is a statement, not an absence: the fixed-point decoder of an
        ESP32-C6 or ESP32-C3 plays the bed and reconstructs no objects. -1 states nothing, which a
        server reads as the board placing whatever the format carries; no build here uses it now.

        The ESP32-P4's 12 is its figure over Sendspin (release image, lane on, null sink, 7.1.4,
        x real time of a 32 ms frame, 2026-10-12): 2 objects 0.41, 4 0.49, 6 0.59, 8 0.68
        (11 bursts late), 12 0.85 (worst burst 1.24), and 15 0.97 with 24 underruns, about 1.5 to
        1.8 ms an object. 8 is the comfortable value and 12 is the margin; 9 to 11 and 13 to 14
        were not run.

        The ESP32-S3's 0 is its figure over Sendspin at 7.1.4, which is the path and the layout a
        Hearth server plays it with (release image sdkconfig.s3-fast, decode lane on, null sink, x
        real time of a 32 ms frame, S3b, 2026-10-12): channel-based 7.1.4 0.78 with no coding
        tool and 0.95 with all, and JOC with 2 objects (the probe's count, LFE bed object
        included) 0.95 with 113 underruns, 3 0.98 with 370, 4 1.13 with 96 late, 5 1.26, 6 1.41,
        enhanced coupling 1.33. No count of objects has a margin there. Over HTTP the same streams
        are 0.15 to 0.25 lower, which is where 4 objects at 0.92 came from, and that is not the
        path a board is played by. With the lane off every figure is worse (3 objects 1.13).
        0 is what a server needs to know: past it a stream with objects goes to the board as PCM
        rendered on the host where the board lists wide enough PCM, and otherwise as its bed.

        The ESP32-P4's 12 is its figure over Sendspin (release image, lane on, null sink, 7.1.4,
        x real time of a 32 ms frame, 2026-10-12): 2 objects 0.41, 4 0.49, 6 0.59, 8 0.68
        (11 bursts late), 12 0.85 (worst burst 1.24), and 15 0.97 with 24 underruns, about 1.5 to
        1.8 ms an object. 8 is the comfortable value and 12 is the margin; 9 to 11 and 13 to 14
        were not run.

        The ESP32-S3's 2 is its release image's (sdkconfig.s3-fast) with the decode lane on, over
        Sendspin, which is the path a Hearth server plays it by. A 32 ms frame of channel-based
        7.1.4 takes 0.77 of real time there with no coding tool and 0.95 with all of them, and each
        object adds about 0.1 (3.1 to 3.7 ms): 2 objects about 0.97, where the demo stream (about
        4 objects) took 1.08 with 72 chunks late, objects-mdct 1.17 with 108 late, height 1.38 with
        190, and a 15-object DEE stream 2.27 (null sink, 2026-10-12). Over HTTP the same streams are
        0.15 to 0.25 lower, which is where 4 objects at 0.92 came from; that figure is not the
        Sendspin path's. Without the lane 4 objects take 1.11 even over HTTP
        (planning/esp32-714-realtime.md).

# THE SENDSPIN PLAYER - main/sendspin.hpp, over the component's player
# (CONFIG_ICLFORGE_SENDSPIN, in the component's own menu). A build with a
# network and the component's player has one; each of these sizes it.
menu "Sendspin player"
    depends on ICLFORGE_SENDSPIN

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_RING_BYTES
    int "Ring of chunks between the session and the decode, bytes"
    default 262144
    range 8192 4194304
    help
        What a server may send ahead of playing it, less a little for stream
        starts and ends, is this: 256 KB is about four and a half seconds of
        a 448 kbit/s E-AC-3 stream's bursts, or 0.9 s of 24-bit stereo PCM.
        In PSRAM when the part has it; a build with none (QEMU) sizes it to
        what its internal RAM can spare.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_MAX_CHUNK_BYTES
    int "The largest chunk taken, bytes"
    default 25600
    range 4096 65535
    help
        An E-AC-3 burst at the bitstream's ceiling is 24,585 bytes with its
        header, and 150 ms of 24-bit stereo PCM 21,613. A shape that plays
        only small streams can take less, and the decode task's buffer for
        one chunk shrinks with it.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_DECODE_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack for the Sendspin decode task, bytes"
    default 32768
    range 12288 65536
    help
        As ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_STACK_BYTES, for the task that decodes a
        Sendspin stream's bursts: the decoder's frames are most of it. The
        page's Sendspin section says what a run left spare.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_DECODE_STACK_IN_PSRAM
    int "Put the Sendspin decode task's stack in PSRAM when present (1) or internal SRAM (0)"
    default 1 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && SPIRAM
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        As ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_DECODE_STACK_IN_PSRAM, for the task that decodes a
        Sendspin stream's bursts. On an ESP32-S3 with Wi-Fi up no internal block
        is over 31,744 bytes by the time the player starts, which is less than
        the stack's default 32,768, so a stack in internal RAM fits only while
        what was allocated before it leaves a block that size: an image that
        started its player fitted by a few bytes, and did not after a few more
        (the lyrics' document and anchors, planning/karaoke-phase2.md, W7d).
        In PSRAM it does not depend on that, and the internal RAM is free for
        the rest. Needs CONFIG_SPIRAM and
        CONFIG_FREERTOS_TASK_CREATE_ALLOW_EXT_MEM; without PSRAM present the
        stack is internal as before.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_SERVER_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack for the Sendspin WebSocket server's task, bytes"
    default 8192
    range 6144 32768
    help
        Every session runs on this task, the Noise handshake's X25519 and
        SHA-256 included. The page's Sendspin section says what a run left
        spare.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_MAX_DELAY_MS
    int "Longest per-output delay, milliseconds (0 = none)"
    default 20
    range 0 100
    help
        The most one speaker can be delayed against another, by a server's settings
        or by the listener's own distances (Preferences, PUT /trim). The delay lines
        are one buffer per output of this length: sixteen outputs of 20 ms is about
        61 KB, in PSRAM when the part has it. 20 ms is 6.86 m of sound's travel, the
        most the nearest and the farthest speaker can be apart; 30 ms would be 10.3 m.
        A build with less holds a shorter span and says so (GET /trim), and a build
        with 0 has levels only.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_AC4
    int "List AC-4 among the Sendspin data types (1) or not (0)"
    default 1 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4 && ICLFORGE_AC4
    default 1 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && ICLFORGE_AC4 && ESP32S3_DATA_CACHE_64KB
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        Whether the board's support object lists `ac4`, so that a server sends it AC-4 as it is
        (one sync frame a chunk) and the board decodes it, up to
        ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_AC4_MAX_CODED_CHANNELS channels and
        ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_AC4_MAX_FRAME_RATE frames a second. Needs ICLFORGE_AC4
        (sdkconfig.ac4); with it off the board lists none and a server decodes AC-4 itself and
        sends PCM.

        The support object states a width and a rate and no codec tool, so the figures are where
        the slowest stream a board is to be sent still keeps up. The ESP32-P4 keeps up with every
        stream it was measured on up to 5.1 (2.0 0.28 to 0.69, 5.1 0.62 to 0.91 with the second
        core, planning/ac4.md D14g to D14i), so it lists AC-4 and states 6 channels, which leaves
        5.1.4 to the server. An ESP32-S3 with the decoder and the 64 KB data cache
        (sdkconfig.s3-fast and sdkconfig.s3-dcache, the s3-n16r8 profile) keeps up with every
        2.0 stream up to 25 frames a second over Sendspin, 0.48 to 0.59 of a frame at frame rate
        index 13 and 0.78 to 0.80 at 24 and 25, and falls behind at 29.97 (0.99, with underruns),
        so it lists AC-4 at 2 channels and 25 frames a second and a server decodes the rest and
        sends PCM. An S3 without the cache does not list AC-4 by default. The ESP32-C6 cannot
        hold the decoder beside Wi-Fi.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_AC4_MAX_CODED_CHANNELS
    int "Widest AC-4 frame decoded in real time: channels (0 = no limit)"
    default 6 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default 2
    range 0 24
    help
        The channel count the support object states for `ac4` (max_coded_channels), counted as the
        decoder puts the presentation out: 2.0 is 2, 5.1 is 6, 5.1.4 is 10. A server sends a
        stream with more as PCM decoded on its own side, and a frame that has more reaches the
        decoder only from a server that did not read the figure, which the board then refuses with
        a console line. Objects are a limit of their own: the board states none placed
        (max_objects of 0), since the decoder hands the player a presentation's channels alone.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_AC4_MAX_FRAME_RATE
    int "Fastest AC-4 stream decoded in real time: frames a second (0 = no limit)"
    default 25 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && ICLFORGE_AC4
    default 0
    range 0 1000
    help
        The rate the support object states for `ac4` (max_frame_rate): a stream whose frames come
        faster is sent as PCM decoded on the server's side. A frame at any rate but the 2,048
        samples of frame rate index 13 goes through the frame-rate converter, which on the
        ESP32-S3 costs a frame about the same at 29.97 a second (33 ms) as at 24 (33 ms), so a
        board that keeps up with 24 and 25 falls behind at 29.97: 0.78 and 0.80 of a frame
        become 0.99. The limit is where a whole
        number of frames a second sits (25 takes 23.976, 24 and 25, 30 takes 29.97 and 30), and
        a frame of a faster stream is refused by the board, which only a server that did not read
        the figure sends. The ESP32-P4 states none: its 29.97 stream took 0.38 of a frame.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_LYRICS_MAX_BYTES
    int "Largest lyric document held for the item playing, bytes (0 = none)"
    default 0 if !SPIRAM
    default 2048 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    default 16384
    range 0 60000
    help
        A server that plays karaoke sends the item's lyrics to a board that
        lists the `lyrics` command, as an LRC text, and then where in the item
        the sound is; the board follows them on its own playout clock and
        reports the line it has the sound in. The document is parsed into
        lines and words, which are many small blocks: measured with Wi-Fi up
        and a stream playing, word-timed lines take about ten times their
        text of heap at the peak, while read, and six times once held (plain
        lines about half that). In internal RAM that is what Wi-Fi's receive
        buffers and the decoder come out of: an S3 was left 27 KB at its
        least with a 64-byte document, 7.8 KB with 2,000 bytes and 0.4 KB
        with 4,000; a P4 223 KB, 182 KB with 4,000 bytes and 66 KB with
        16,000; a C6's Wi-Fi receive buffers failed to allocate at 2,000.
        So a part with PSRAM reads and holds the document there
        (main/sendspin/player/psram_scope.hpp), which leaves the message as
        it arrives (a few times its size, for a moment) and the receive
        buffer that keeps the room of the largest message: an S3 with 5.1
        playing was left 27 KB at its least with a 64-byte document, 19.5 KB
        with 2,000 bytes, 9.3 KB with 8,000, and 16,000 broke the play. The
        defaults are what each part spares: none without PSRAM, 2,048 on an
        S3, 16,384 otherwise. A server keeps its message within the largest
        chunk taken above as well, so the smaller of the two is what it
        sends, and a document that does not fit is sent as none. The board
        takes a document only while three times its size (twelve times of
        internal RAM where PSRAM has no room) and 8 KB of internal heap are
        free, and otherwise holds none and says why on its console. 0 lists
        neither command and holds nothing.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_LEAD_MS
    int "Lead a stream's first chunk needs, milliseconds (required_lead_time_ms)"
    default 250
    range 50 30000
    help
        From a chunk's arrival to its first sample leaving the port: the
        decoder's one-frame hold, a burst's decode and render, and the DMA
        queue, with room to spare.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_BUFFER_MS
    int "Audio kept buffered while playing, milliseconds (min_buffer_ms)"
    default 300
    range 0 30000

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_UNPAIRED_ACCESS
    int "Let an approved server play without pairing (1) or not (0)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        pairing.md's unpaired access, which a player offers and a server's
        operator approves. Off by default: a board plays only for servers it
        has paired with.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_SENDSPIN_PCM
    int "Offer player@v1 with PCM (1), for Music Assistant, or only _iclforge_player@v1 (0)"
    default 1
    range 0 1

endmenu

menu "Panel (lyrics on a screen)"

# The board's screen (planning/karaoke-p4x-panel.md). A directory CMake picks, like the sink and
# the source (main/CMakeLists.txt, main/panel/panel.hpp). Read from CMake, never from C++.
choice ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL
    prompt "Panel"
    default ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_NONE
    help
        None draws nothing and costs nothing: every image but the one for a
        board with a screen. A frame buffer is LVGL drawing into 1.2 MB of
        PSRAM with no glass behind it: what runs on a board that has no panel
        (a FireBeetle 2) to see what the screen looks like and what drawing
        it costs, read back over HTTP (ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_CAPTURE_PORT).
        A MIPI DSI panel is the same drawing on a 1024 by 600 panel over the
        P4's DSI host, with touch, as on Espressif's ESP32-P4X-Function-EV-Board
        (see ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_BOARD_* for its pins).

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_NONE
    bool "None"

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_NULL
    bool "A frame buffer with no panel behind it (ESP32-P4, needs PSRAM)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4 && SPIRAM

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_DSI
    bool "A MIPI DSI panel with touch (ESP32-P4, needs PSRAM)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4 && SPIRAM

endchoice

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_WIDTH
    int "Panel width, pixels"
    default 1024
    range 320 1920

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_HEIGHT
    int "Panel height, pixels"
    default 600
    range 240 1200

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_FPS
    int "Frames a second while a word is being filled"
    default 30
    range 5 60
    help
        The fill moves with the sound, so it is redrawn this often. Between
        words and in a gap the screen asks for a tenth of this.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_TASK_PRIORITY
    int "Priority of the panel's task"
    default 2
    range 1 10
    help
        Below the decode task and the I2S writer (the audio is the product),
        and above the idle task. The screen is a guest: when the cores are
        busy it is drawn late rather than the audio being.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_YIELD
    int "The audio first: 0 nothing, 1 hold the Meters view at ten a second while the decoder is busy, 2 that and the screen's task on the decode core"
    default 1
    range 0 2
    help
        A decoder that spends most of its 32 ms on a burst (a 7.1.4 object
        stream, AC-4) is slowed by the screen's drawing and analysis running
        beside it, whatever their priority. On the kit the Meters view cost
        a burst 1.6 to 1.8 ms under JOC 7.1.4 and 1.5 ms under AC-4 5.1; 1
        holds it at ten updates a second while the decoder is busy and the
        cost falls to 0.9 to 1.2 and 1.1 ms (a steady ten, 2 to 3 % of the
        steps without an update). 2 also makes the panel task on the decode
        core (always: FreeRTOS here cannot move a task between cores), where
        it can only run while the decode task sleeps: 0.5 to 0.7 and 0.65 ms,
        but the view then misses 11 to 16 % of its steps and every other
        view under JOC refreshes in twice the time (planning/p4x-meters-view.md,
        "What the screen costs the audio path"). 0 leaves the screen as it
        was. The audio comes first, so the default is 1.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_YIELD_BURST_US
    int "A mean burst this long, microseconds, is a busy decoder"
    default 14000
    range 1000 30000
    help
        Read once a second from the player's mean burst; in at this, out at
        70 % of it. A 5.1 E-AC-3 burst is 4 ms, JOC 7.1.4 21 ms, AC-4 5.1 26.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_STACK_BYTES
    int "Stack of the panel's task, bytes (PSRAM)"
    default 16384
    range 8192 65536

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_LINE_BUFFER_ROWS
    int "Rows of the partial render buffers (two, PSRAM)"
    default 40
    range 10 200
    help
        LVGL draws a dirty area through two buffers of this many rows of the
        panel's width, in RGB565: 80 KB each at 1024 wide and 40 rows.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_DMA2D_FLUSH
    bool "Copy each render buffer into the DSI frame buffer with the 2D-DMA"
    default y
    depends on ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_DSI
    help
        Without this ESP-IDF copies each rendered area into the frame buffer
        on the CPU, line by line, PSRAM to PSRAM, and tells LVGL it is done
        before returning: the panel task is held for the copy and the second
        render buffer never overlaps it. With it the copy is a 2D-DMA job and
        LVGL is told from the interrupt that ends it, so the task draws the
        next chunk meanwhile. The jobs read and write PSRAM, as the CPU did.
        If the DMA cannot be set up the CPU copy stays.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_CAPTURE_PORT
    int "Port that serves the picture as a BMP at /panel.bmp (0 = none)"
    default 0
    range 0 65535
    help
        A second, tiny HTTP server for development: GET /panel.bmp is what is
        on the glass (or in the frame buffer, with no glass), GET /panel is
        the drawing statistics as JSON, POST /panel/tap?x=..&y=.. is a tap
        (a touch with no touch controller) and POST /panel/demo?mode=.. plays
        a demo song. It has no authentication: anyone on the network can read
        the screen and press its buttons. It is how a design is checked before
        the panel is here, and what the screen tests drive a board by.

        0, the default, starts no server. A release image has 0
        (profiles.json locks it, and package_firmware.py refuses an image
        built with any other). 8081 comes from the panel-capture overlay
        (sdkconfig.panel-capture) for an image that is run on a bench, and
        from sdkconfig.panel-null, which exists to be read back.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_BACKLIGHT_GPIO
    int "Backlight PWM GPIO (-1 = none)"
    default 26
    range -1 54
    help
        Espressif's ESP32-P4X-Function-EV-Board with its 7-inch panel: GPIO26
        for the backlight, GPIO27 for the panel's reset (both through
        jumpers on the LCD adapter board). Check against the schematic of
        the board the image is for.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_RESET_GPIO
    int "Panel reset GPIO (-1 = none)"
    default 27
    range -1 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_I2C_SDA_GPIO
    int "Touch controller I2C SDA GPIO"
    default 7
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_I2C_SCL_GPIO
    int "Touch controller I2C SCL GPIO"
    default 8
    range 0 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_TOUCH_INT_GPIO
    int "Touch controller interrupt GPIO (-1 = polled)"
    default -1
    range -1 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_TOUCH_RST_GPIO
    int "Touch controller reset GPIO (-1 = none)"
    default -1
    range -1 54

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_TOUCH_SWAP_XY
    int "Touch controller's x and y are the other way round (0 or 1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_TOUCH_MIRROR_X
    int "Touch controller's x runs right to left (0 or 1)"
    default 1
    range 0 1
    help
        The touch layer of the ESP32-P4X-Function-EV-Board's 7-inch panel is mounted turned half a
        turn from its display: a press at the bottom right is reported at the top left. The first run
        on the kit found it (a tap on the Adjust button did nothing), and 1 here and for the y axis
        undoes it. Another board's profile sets what its own touch layer needs.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_TOUCH_MIRROR_Y
    int "Touch controller's y runs bottom to top (0 or 1)"
    default 1
    range 0 1

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_DSI_LDO_CHANNEL
    int "LDO channel that powers the DSI PHY (-1 = already on)"
    default 3
    range -1 4
    help
        The P4's MIPI DSI PHY is supplied from an on-chip LDO channel, 3 at
        2.5 V on the ESP32-P4X-Function-EV-Board.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_PANEL_DSI_LDO_MV
    int "That LDO's voltage, millivolts"
    default 2500
    range 500 3300

# What a person chooses once and leaves (the Preferences screen, planning/p4x-preferences.md), as the board
# has it before anyone has chosen: NVS holds the choice, and a QEMU run or a new board uses these. Read by every
# build (settings.cpp) and acted on by the one with a panel.
config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_BRIGHTNESS_PERCENT
    int "Backlight when lit, percent (10 to 100)"
    default 80
    range 10 100
    help
        The level of the backlight while the screen is lit. The dimmed screen goes to a floor of 4 percent
        (never above this), and the dark view turns it off.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_CLOCK_FORMAT
    int "Clock view: 24 hours (0), 12 hours (1), 24 hours with seconds (2), 12 hours with seconds (3)"
    default 0
    range 0 3
    help
        Bit 0 is the 12-hour clock and bit 1 the seconds: the stored value of iclforge::wallclock::ClockFormat.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NIGHT_SCHEDULE
    string "Night schedule: two times by the board's own time, then on or off"
    default "22:00-07:00 off"
    help
        Go dark between the two times (HH:MM-HH:MM, across midnight when the second is earlier), as the dark view
        is: a touch lights the screen for a while. The form PUT /night-schedule takes. A board that does not
        know the time is lit whatever this says.

config ICLFORGE_EXAMPLE_NIGHT_IDLE
    int "Night schedule also darkens the idle screen (0 or 1)"
    default 0
    range 0 1
    help
        Whether the night schedule covers the screen shown between songs as well as the one shown while a song
        with no lyrics plays. Off until the owner decides (planning/p4x-now-playing.md, "Open").

endmenu

endmenu
